WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:04.360
Let's say you get a windfall thanks to your
excellent contributions to your employer like

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:10.179
me, or even inherited a chunk of change from
your distant uncle, or you found a few grand

00:00:10.179 --> 00:00:12.180
in a suitcase on a side of the highway, whatever.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.639
You got some extra bread.

00:00:13.639 --> 00:00:17.270
Let's figure out what to do with that extra
scratch.

00:00:17.270 --> 00:00:19.570
It's not easy to make sense of your finances.

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:23.470
I'm Shahien Nasiripour, and I want to meet
people with an obsession to beat the system

00:00:23.470 --> 00:00:35.410
and never leave money on the table.

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:38.480
And who better to tell me what to do with
my money than a finance expert?

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.280
And luckily enough, I've got three of them
right here.

00:00:42.280 --> 00:00:45.920
Hi Shahien.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:48.250
So we've got some extra money burning holes
in our pockets.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:49.590
What should we be thinking about?

00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:51.690
Do you have toxic credit card debt?

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:54.020
Attack that first.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:58.270
It's your double-digit interest rates.

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:00.710
It's moving you backwards very quickly.

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:02.290
Please get rid of that.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:03.460
Don't even think about it.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:08.850
If you've got a 15% credit card or a 23% credit
card, and at the same time you've got a three

00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:13.429
or 4% student loan hanging out there, we might
be looking at your credit cards first and

00:01:13.429 --> 00:01:16.630
think about where those dollars are best spent
going forward.

00:01:16.630 --> 00:01:19.240
Right now, we're concerned about the highest
costing dollar.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:25.340
Your credit card is a debt that is typically
based on money that you've already spent.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:26.340
Your finished.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:27.840
It's time to pay off the debt.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.069
You'll hear financial planners often talk
about an emergency fund.

00:01:32.069 --> 00:01:36.939
Then you'll hear them all say, "Three to six
months is what you need."

00:01:36.939 --> 00:01:37.939
Three to six months.

00:01:37.939 --> 00:01:41.299
Three to six months of your living expenses
in cash.

00:01:41.299 --> 00:01:46.319
So the most important thing about portfolio
construction is understanding which risks

00:01:46.319 --> 00:01:53.539
your portfolio is open to.

00:01:53.539 --> 00:02:00.009
So if you bought 100% of one individual stock
and then that business goes out of business,

00:02:00.009 --> 00:02:01.380
you go to zero.

00:02:01.380 --> 00:02:02.789
That's go to zero risk.

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:05.469
Volatility, drawdowns, economic events.

00:02:05.469 --> 00:02:06.689
This is part of the game.

00:02:06.689 --> 00:02:12.820
If you're not equipped to handle the inevitabilities
of markets going down and economic cycles

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:15.470
turning over, you better reassess your situation.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:19.510
So you don't have to be emotionally impacted
by these types of events.

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:27.580
Ideally, these will be diversified mutual
funds, index funds, diversification, diversification,

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:28.680
diversification.

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:32.769
Make sure that you do have some fixed income
in the portfolio even though fixed income

00:02:32.769 --> 00:02:36.680
is not going to give you the rate of return
that stock market does.

00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:39.879
It serves as a temper in your portfolio.

00:02:39.879 --> 00:02:42.099
It reduces the volatility.

00:02:42.099 --> 00:02:45.319
It's not there to make you rich, but it's
there to help you sleep at night.

00:02:45.319 --> 00:02:49.769
How much volatility are we talking about when
it comes to what allows you to sleep at night?

00:02:49.769 --> 00:02:54.489
If your bonus has a sizable tax impact or
even takes you into a higher tax bracket,

00:02:54.489 --> 00:02:59.040
check to see if your employer has a deferred
compensation plan.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:04.659
The advantage of that is that your compensation,
this bonus that you'll receive is not taxed

00:03:04.659 --> 00:03:12.030
too, but goes into an account which will only
be taxed to you when you begin taking the

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:14.090
funds out.

00:03:14.090 --> 00:03:20.579
So you've paid off your debt and built a solid
rainy day fund.

00:03:20.579 --> 00:03:21.579
Bravo.

00:03:21.579 --> 00:03:23.190
Now you're ready to invest.

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:26.989
If you're anything like me and terrified about
managing your money, you may want to hire

00:03:26.989 --> 00:03:29.950
someone like Douglas, Erica, or Ian.

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:33.890
Let's ask Bloomberg Reporter Suzanne Woolley
about what we should be looking for if we're

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:36.379
looking to hire a financial advisor.

00:03:36.379 --> 00:03:41.290
Fees, sometimes, we might not pay so much
attention to it because sometimes they don't

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:43.110
seem like they're that big.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:48.209
Over the years, they really can mushroom and
eat away at your investment, particularly

00:03:48.209 --> 00:03:50.790
if we're going into a slower growth environment.

00:03:50.790 --> 00:04:02.849
A lot of people like fee-only planners, and
part of that is just because it's so transparent.

00:04:02.849 --> 00:04:04.180
You want to know what you're paying.

00:04:04.180 --> 00:04:09.389
The real key is just to be very upfront and
ask your planner, "What are your fees?

00:04:09.389 --> 00:04:10.749
How they structure?"

00:04:10.749 --> 00:04:18.640
Whether they are a fiduciary.

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:26.450
This means that they have to sell you the
product that is in your best interest.

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:30.860
Maybe it's time for a financial tuneup, and
for you, maybe that's saving cash or paying

00:04:30.860 --> 00:04:32.250
down debt.

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:36.200
But personal finance is personal, so see what's
right for you.

